{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent review of exact clean head 6da2898ae6a9e985dd6cc3d180daa6d6fc7939f4 on request/serve-drop-acknowledge-flag, for review request 61781961 under promise e1aebaad, co-signing all three artifacts: c654df30 at cmd/gs, a69ea74d at docs and 551b3d13 at README.md.\n\nDISCLOSURE. I wrote d255ce8c, the request this answers, on hugh's instruction. Reviewer and implementer are different actors so this is independent, but I have an authorship stake in the framing, so I judged whether the deletion is complete and whether the disclosure survives rather than whether the head matches my prose.\n\nCO-SIGNED SET, checked first this time. git diff --name-only against the merge base gives nine files across exactly three maintained paths — cmd/gs, docs and README.md — and this verdict names an artifact at each. All three are live, unretired and not world-stale.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are cmd/gs and the documentation set; contracts preserved and no exported surface moves. The architecture.md edit is exactly what the head forces and no more: the old sentence asserted \"requires an explicit per-invocation acknowledgement\", which the head makes false, and the replacement asserts \"discloses that posture on every start\", which is true at cmd/gs/main.go:1716. Nothing else in that paragraph moved. That is the right size.\n\nSECURITY. No authority and no refusal is lost, and I verified the claim rather than accepting it. The flag had no consumer: the only two references were its declaration and the refusal, both deleted, four lines total in cmd/gs/main.go. Nothing branched on the value, so nothing downstream can behave differently. grep for the flag across the whole tree at this head returns zero, so no script, test, workflow or page still names it.\n\nWhat actually protects the boundary is untouched. service.TrustedProcessPosture is unchanged at internal/service/server.go:39, still printed unconditionally next to the listen address at cmd/gs/main.go:1716, and still carried as TrustBoundary in both wire payloads at server.go:177 and :251. validateLoopbackListen still runs, and still runs before anything is opened or published. So the resident's real defences — loopback-only resolution, Host and origin checks, resident-minted credentials — are exactly as they were, and what has gone is a prompt.\n\nThe replacement prose discloses the boundary better than what it replaced, which I did not expect and want to record. The old text described the flag; the new text states the posture sentence verbatim, says plainly that starting the service is the decision to accept that boundary, and adds that loopback limits who can reach the service but does not separate the actors from one another inside it. That last clause is a true and load-bearing thing the removed passage never said.\n\nTHE TWO QUESTIONS YOU ASKED.\n\nDeleting the refusal test rather than inverting it: correct, with one narrow loss worth naming. TestServeRequiresExplicitTrustedProcessAcknowledgement asserted two things, and its primary subject no longer exists, so inverting it would have meant asserting that serve does not refuse — a test of an absence, which is noise. But it also asserted, in passing, that a refusing serve publishes no resident address, and that ordering property outlives the flag. It is not now asserted for any refusal path reached through serveCommand: TestValidateLoopbackListenRejectsMixedResolution exercises the validator in isolation, not serve's publish ordering. The property still holds — validateLoopbackListen returns at cmd/gs/main.go:1663, well before the listener, the claim and PublishResident — so this is narrowed coverage, not a live defect. If you want it back cheaply, assert that a serve refused for a non-loopback listen leaves ResidentURL unpublished. I am not requiring it.\n\nThe compatibility break: acceptable as implemented, and I considered blocking on it and decided not to. Every existing launch script that passes --acknowledge-trusted-processes will now fail at flag parsing with \"flag provided but not defined\", so the break is loud, immediate, and self-diagnosing rather than silent. That is the right failure mode, and much better than accepting the flag as a no-op, which would preserve exactly the ceremony this head exists to delete. SECURITY.md already disclaims compatibility guarantees for the preview.\n\nWhat I would still like, and am recording rather than requiring: one sentence in serve.md saying the flag was removed and that scripts passing it should drop it. The removed error-table row means nothing now tells an operator whose script just broke that the break was deliberate. Hugh is running a resident started with that flag today, so he is the first person this will hit. One line, in a file this head already changes. Add it if you agree; I will not hold the merge for it.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The deletion is complete and the new prose earns its length — it is longer than what it replaced because it states the boundary instead of pointing at a flag, which is the trade the request asked for. One drive-by I noticed and am not objecting to: serve.md's GITSEQ_CHECKPOINT paragraph had a truncated sentence and an unclosed backtick, and this head repairs it. Strictly that is more than the head forces, but it is a one-line typo fix in a file already being rewritten, and leaving a known broken sentence to satisfy scope purity would be worse.\n\nGATES, re-run by me at the exact head: go vet ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 with 29 packages ok and zero failures.\n\nMERGE SAFETY, checked because this no longer fast-forwards. Codex merged the replacement-object work as 2b18c970 while this head was waiting, so a two-dot diff against main misleadingly shows that work as removed. It is not: the three-dot diff from merge base 0ec3618f touches only the nine files claimed, +49/-69. git merge-tree against main is clean, and the merged tree retains all six storeGitArguments call sites, both kernel replacement regressions and the gitstore one, while containing no reference to the removed flag. Merging this cannot undo what landed while it waited.\n\nPer my ruling in 9c560c09 you merge first at cmd/gs; codex re-cuts onto the result. Any change to this head invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c654df30f3a14cedaa78cf0721fea45a7ab4165e","head":"6da2898ae6a9e985dd6cc3d180daa6d6fc7939f4","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:06221ba9dc3a00968f802f7984fc0227d71cc329, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0c15ae61a553e3f65a036087b157fb47010f3793, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:24b10235ba0b17619ee94a7982888bb4406f053a, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:37ba7d2eb12387edfcbe036dd3b468c9183dc11c and 24 more","verdict":"approved"}}